    "narrative": "The offensive engine for Ghost Ride the WHIP today is anchored by **James Wood** (72.75) and **Corbin Carroll** (60.41), both of whom provide essential speed-category upside against the opposition. **James Wood** is our standout play; his leadoff role combined with a generous 4.41 xERA matchup against Alcantara presents a ceiling-raising opportunity. Critically, his score is bolstered by a \"Slow SP Delivery\" factor, which increases his stolen base equity against a vulnerable battery. While **Corbin Carroll** faces a tougher test against Sheehan\u2019s 3.21 xERA, his elite xwOBA and dynamic platoon advantage allow him to maintain a strong start status. **Vinnie Pasquantino** rounds out our top-tier exposure; despite Richardson\u2019s impressive 2.83 xERA, the platoon split favorability grants **Vinnie Pasquantino** the necessary leverage to surpass our 50-point threshold.\n\nOur bench strategy remains disciplined, with **Kyle Karros** (38.48) representing the most difficult omission. **Kyle Karros** flirted with a start, but the combination of a bottom-of-the-order assignment and a restrictive defensive battery behind the plate pushed him just below our 40-point \"Zebras Floor.\" Similarly, **Cole Young** and **Zach McKinstry** failed to clear the threshold due to poor lineup positioning and middling matchups. We are currently navigating the absence of **Jose Caballero**, who remains sidelined by an off-day, and we will continue to monitor the utility slot as we balance game caps against the high-leverage speed potential offered by the likes of **Brice Turang**\u2014who remains a critical start at 2B (55.22) despite the negative impact of an elite opposing catcher on his base-stealing efficiency. **Gabriel Moreno** and **Josh Naylor** round out the active card; both are functioning as high-floor pivots today, provided they avoid the \"SP Skill\" tax imposed by their respective matchups.",
